data
  title The four tradeoff axes — every choice buys one property by spending another
  desc Naming the price, not the choice, is the senior signal. State what you bought and what it cost.
  lists
    - label Consistency vs availability — the CAP/PACELC trade
      desc Strong consistency means coordination — it costs availability under partition and latency always. Eventual consistency buys availability and low latency at the price of stale reads. A like count can be stale; a bank balance cannot.
    - label Latency vs cost
      desc Lower latency buys you more replicas, more cache, more regions — all money. You can hit almost any target by spending enough; the design question is the cheapest way to hit the target you actually need.
    - label Simplicity vs scale
      desc The simplest design that meets the requirements wins. A single Postgres instance that fits the load beats a sharded multi-region system you did not need. Add complexity only where an estimate forces it.
    - label Read vs write optimization
      desc Precompute on write to make reads cheap (fan-out-on-write), or compute on read to make writes cheap (fan-out-on-read) — but not both. The access pattern's read/write ratio decides which.
